# rb1_locality.py seed=20261018 (referee B, independent of checks/p1_locality.py)

== A. semimeasure and three-valued conventions: cumulative loss along every reveal order (with repeats)
   equals -log nu(D) + log nu(empty)  [T1.1(b),(c)]
   two-valued: 121 instances, 487 (instance, order) pairs incl. random repeats; max deviation 4.44e-16 bits
   three-valued: 117 instances, all orders; conditionals over {1,0,i} sum to nu(D_t-1) exactly (asserted); max deviation 4.44e-16 bits

== B. normalised convention [T1.2]: loss = -log nu(D) + log nu(empty) + sum_t log(1 - I_t); order and
   off-event mass matter
   1137 random instances, all orders: max |normalised loss - formula| = 8.88e-16 bits
   largest order spread found: 2.4594 bits (normalised losses over orders: 2.4594, 2.4594, 0.0000, 0.0000, 0.0000, 0.0000)
      instance: N=5 atoms, truth 4, machine [('2/25', ['0b11011', '0b10100']), ('6/25', ['0b111', '0b100', '0b1', '0b11110']), ('4/25', ['0b1110']), ('6/25', ['0b10011']), ('1/5', ['0b1111']), ('2/25', ['0b10011'])], data {'0b10000': 1, '0b10010': 1, '0b11101': 1}
   silent elsewhere   nu(empty)=1 nu(D)=1/2 | semimeasure loss 1.0000 | normalised loss -0.0000 bits
   refutes elsewhere  nu(empty)=1 nu(D)=1/2 | semimeasure loss 1.0000 | normalised loss 1.0000 bits
   mixed              nu(empty)=1 nu(D)=1/2 | semimeasure loss 1.0000 | normalised loss 0.5850 bits

== C. joint-cell identity and partitions [T1.3, T1.4] for arbitrary (non-interval) sentences
   2000 instances: max |nu(D) - nu(prove C_D)| = 0 (exact); sum over cells of beta <= nu(empty) in all (406 equal, 1594 strict); equality characterised correctly in 2000 of 2000

== D. random questions (dithered grid), exact integration over the dither [T1.5]
   D1 separation probability of x=0, y=d equals min(|d|/Delta,1) at 8 distances: max error 2.8e-17
   D2 300 random discrete forecasts supported in J (|J| <= Delta, x in J): max |S_mu - CRIGN/Delta| = 5.55e-16 bits
   D3 near-point forecasts P = (1-s) delta_y + s Unif(64 atoms at -31.5..31.5 step 1), x = 0, Delta = 1
      d=0.25 (sep 0.25): s=0.01: S=3.1717, S/log2(1/s)=0.4774; s=0.0001: S=4.8220, S/log2(1/s)=0.3629; s=1e-08: S=8.1439, S/log2(1/s)=0.3064
      d=0.50 (sep 0.50): s=0.01: S=6.3291, S/log2(1/s)=0.9526; s=0.0001: S=9.6439, S/log2(1/s)=0.7258; s=1e-08: S=16.2877, S/log2(1/s)=0.6129
      d=2.00 (sep 1.00): s=0.01: S=12.6439, S/log2(1/s)=1.9031; s=0.0001: S=19.2877, S/log2(1/s)=1.4515; s=1e-08: S=32.5754, S/log2(1/s)=1.2258
   D4 author's example, truth 0: near = Unif{-2..2}, scattered = Unif{-20,-10,0,10,20}
      Delta= 1.0: dithered: near 2.3219, scattered 2.3219 | FIXED grid with cell [-Delta/2, Delta/2): near 2.3219, scattered 2.3219 | fixed grid, worst offset for near: 2.3219
      Delta= 2.0: dithered: near 1.3219, scattered 2.3219 | FIXED grid with cell [-Delta/2, Delta/2): near 1.3219, scattered 2.3219 | fixed grid, worst offset for near: 1.3219
      Delta= 5.0: dithered: near 0.4236, scattered 2.3219 | FIXED grid with cell [-Delta/2, Delta/2): near -0.0000, scattered 2.3219 | fixed grid, worst offset for near: 0.7370
      Delta=10.0: dithered: near 0.2118, scattered 2.3219 | FIXED grid with cell [-Delta/2, Delta/2): near -0.0000, scattered 2.3219 | fixed grid, worst offset for near: 0.7370
      -> a FIXED question family (cells of width 5, one fixed offset) also ranks the near forecast better (0 vs 2.3219 bits);
         the dithered loss is the average over offsets of fixed-grid losses (it is a mixture of partitions).
   D5 Gaussian-shaped forecasts N(y, 1) (discretised on a 0.001 grid over y +- 12), truth x = 0, dithered grid
      Delta=0.1: miss: S(miss)-S(0) [miss^2/(2 ln2)] = 0: 0.000 [0.000]; 1: 0.721 [0.721]; 2: 2.883 [2.885]; 4: 11.532 [11.542]; 6: 25.947 [25.969]
      Delta=1.0: miss: S(miss)-S(0) [miss^2/(2 ln2)] = 0: 0.000 [0.000]; 1: 0.664 [0.721]; 2: 2.661 [2.885]; 4: 10.708 [11.542]; 6: 24.272 [25.969]
      Delta=4.0: miss: S(miss)-S(0) [miss^2/(2 ln2)] = 0: 0.000 [0.000]; 1: 0.294 [0.721]; 2: 1.252 [2.885]; 4: 5.958 [11.542]; 6: 15.519 [25.969]
      -> the expected loss grows like (miss)^2/(2 sigma^2 ln 2) for large misses at EVERY Delta: a fixed or random
         question family does not bound the grading of misses once the forecast has Gaussian shape; T1.5(d) is a
         statement about near-point forecasts only.

